WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.099
Leading Architecture at Scale.

2
00:00:02.379 --> 00:00:06.582
Chapter 37, from the Architect part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:08.332 --> 00:00:09.292
At a glance:

4
00:00:09.572 --> 00:00:10.745
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.028
Reader bands:

6
00:00:12.308 --> 00:00:14.697
B3, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:14.977 --> 00:00:20.439
Ch 16, Ch 31, Ch 32, Ch 33, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:39.855
The socio-technical side of architecture, how team structure shapes the system (Conway's law), how to organise teams around the flow of change, how a platform team keeps a fleet of services on a paved road, how to run a fleet-wide migration, and how staff+ engineers lead without formal authority.

9
00:00:40.135 --> 00:00:44.316
Four runnable labs over the grocery platform's 26 services.

10
00:00:45.666 --> 00:00:46.498
L1:

11
00:00:46.778 --> 00:00:48.101
Conway's law, measured.

12
00:00:48.381 --> 00:01:10.397
A year of 500 features against two ownership maps: organised by history and layer, 91% of features needed more than one team and every bounded context was split across 2 to 3 teams; aligning teams to the Ch 33 contexts helped only modestly (86%), because this year's work flows across those contexts.

13
00:01:10.677 --> 00:01:12.661
L2: teams from change data.

14
00:01:12.941 --> 00:01:34.658
Within cognitive-load limits (2 to 4 services per team, balanced load), grouping services by how they change together cut hand-offs from 500 to 240 and multi-team features from 86% to 43%, and surfaced groupings to question, such as a shared messaging capability that belongs with a platform team.

15
00:01:36.008 --> 00:01:37.928
L3: the paved road, scanned.

16
00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:58.539
26 real repos generated from template v3 or v2 or v1 or by hand and scanned for 8 golden-path capabilities: current-template services passed 100%, v2 60%, v1 28%, hand-rolled 19%; only 8 of 26 were fully on the current path.

17
00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.888
L4: a fleet-wide migration.

18
00:02:01.168 --> 00:02:24.336
Spring Boot 2, then 3 across 26 services: a central team took 31 sprints (~14 months); team-owned with libraries migrated first 11; starting everyone before the libraries were ready finished about the same (10.4) but spent 32% more effort on workarounds; adding codemods 8.6 sprints and 37% less effort.

19
00:02:25.686 --> 00:02:26.667
Reading time:

20
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:28.739
110 min + code.

21
00:02:30.089 --> 00:02:30.943
Part 1.

22
00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:33.143
Introduction and Why It Matters.

23
00:02:34.393 --> 00:02:37.465
A story: the architecture that the org chart rewrote.

24
00:02:37.745 --> 00:02:56.070
The grocery platform's architecture team produced an excellent target architecture: bounded contexts from event storming (Ch 33), a modular monolith for ordering, services for inventory and fulfilment, events between contexts, cells for blast radius (Ch 34).

25
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:02.046
The ADRs were written, the diagrams generated from a model, the fitness functions in CI.

26
00:03:02.326 --> 00:03:05.654
Eighteen months later the system looked nothing like the target.

27
00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:08.110
The reason was not bad engineering.

28
00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.841
It was the organisation:

29
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:18.572
Teams were organised by technical layer and history, an API team, a data team, a core team, a mobile team.

30
00:03:18.852 --> 00:03:22.073
Every bounded context was split across two or three of them.

31
00:03:22.353 --> 00:03:33.916
Each feature needed a meeting between teams, so teams built integration shortcuts, a shared table here, a synchronous call there, and the architecture drifted towards the org chart (L1).

32
00:03:34.196 --> 00:03:37.695
The architecture team decided; delivery teams implemented.

33
00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:43.713
Decisions made far from the code were resisted, misunderstood, or quietly worked around.

34
00:03:43.993 --> 00:03:47.577
The architects became a review bottleneck and were cut out of the loop.

35
00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:51.210
Standards were documents, not defaults.

36
00:03:51.490 --> 00:04:00.685
Every new service was set up by hand from a wiki page, so each one was different: some had tracing, some had the outbox, some had JSON logs.

37
00:04:00.965 --> 00:04:04.271
By service twenty, no two were configured alike (L3).

38
00:04:04.551 --> 00:04:06.386
The big migration never finished.

39
00:04:06.666 --> 00:04:11.039
A move to Spring Boot 3 was assigned to a two-person migration squad.

40
00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:19.725
A year later a third of the services were migrated, the squad was burnt out, and the owning teams had learned nothing about their new stack (L4).

41
00:04:21.075 --> 00:04:23.464
Nobody could say whether things were getting better.

42
00:04:23.744 --> 00:04:29.909
There were no measures of delivery flow, coordination cost, or standards adoption, only anecdotes.

43
00:04:30.189 --> 00:04:36.589
The recovery was led by two principal engineers who treated the organisation as part of the architecture.

44
00:04:36.869 --> 00:04:53.936
They realigned teams to how work actually flowed, created a platform team whose product was a paved road, replaced review gates with an advice process and automated checks, and ran migrations as sequenced, team-owned programmes with the platform doing the enabling work first.

45
00:04:55.286 --> 00:04:55.969
Quote:

46
00:04:56.249 --> 00:04:57.913
The thesis of this chapter.

47
00:04:58.193 --> 00:05:07.451
At scale, architecture is socio-technical: the system you get is shaped as much by team boundaries, incentives and defaults as by diagrams.

48
00:05:07.731 --> 00:05:17.033
Conway's law isn't a curiosity, it's a force, and you either use it (align teams to how change flows) or it rewrites your architecture for you.

49
00:05:17.313 --> 00:05:34.038
The architect's leverage therefore shifts from making decisions to shaping the conditions under which many teams make good decisions: team boundaries that minimise hand-offs, a platform that makes the right thing the easy thing, governance by guardrails and advice rather than gates,

50
00:05:34.318 --> 00:05:39.822
migrations that are planned and enabled rather than assigned, and measures that show whether it's working.

51
00:05:41.172 --> 00:05:43.476
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

52
