81
00:06:51.442 --> 00:06:52.765
Learning objectives.

82
00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.943
By the end you will be able to:

83
00:06:55.223 --> 00:06:59.959
Explain and use Conway's law, including the inverse Conway manoeuvre (L1).

84
00:07:00.239 --> 00:07:07.301
Design team boundaries with Team Topologies' team types, interaction modes and cognitive-load limits (L2).

85
00:07:07.581 --> 00:07:13.106
Build and run a paved road as a platform product, and measure adoption and drift (L3).

86
00:07:13.386 --> 00:07:16.757
Plan and lead fleet-wide migrations that finish (L4).

87
00:07:18.107 --> 00:07:22.011
Govern architecture across many teams without becoming a bottleneck.

88
00:07:22.291 --> 00:07:28.691
Lead as a staff+ engineer: technical strategy, influence without authority, and measuring outcomes.

89
00:07:30.041 --> 00:07:30.915
Part 2.

90
00:07:31.195 --> 00:07:33.073
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

91
00:07:34.323 --> 00:07:37.288
Intuition: architecture is socio-technical.

92
00:07:37.568 --> 00:07:43.221
Every interface in a system is also a line of communication between the people who own each side.

93
00:07:43.501 --> 00:07:55.981
If two teams must change their services together every week, they must talk every week; if they can't, they'll find ways around it, shared databases, duplicated logic, synchronous calls just for now.

94
00:07:56.261 --> 00:08:01.701
Over time the system's structure converges on the organisation's communication structure.

95
00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:04.925
That makes three things part of an architect's job at scale:

96
00:08:05.205 --> 00:08:11.520
Team boundaries, who owns what determines how much coordination each change needs (L1, L2).

97
00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:17.990
Defaults, what's easy gets done; what requires remembering a wiki page doesn't (L3).

98
00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:25.481
Flow of work, large changes across many teams need sequencing, enablers and ownership, not heroics (L4).

99
00:08:26.831 --> 00:08:29.433
Conway's law and the inverse Conway manoeuvre.

100
00:08:29.713 --> 00:08:38.396
Organizations which design systems are constrained to produce designs which are copies of the communication structures of these organizations.

101
00:08:38.676 --> 00:08:41.236
Melvin Conway, 1968.

102
00:08:41.516 --> 00:08:42.945
Two practical readings:

103
00:08:43.225 --> 00:08:48.751
Descriptive: look at your org chart and you can predict your architecture's seams, and its problems.

104
00:08:49.031 --> 00:08:56.476
The §1.1 platform, organised by layer, grew layer-shaped services with cross-team coupling in every feature.

105
00:08:57.826 --> 00:09:08.706
Prescriptive, the inverse Conway manoeuvre: design the team structure you need for the architecture you want, so the natural communication paths reinforce the intended boundaries.

106
00:09:10.056 --> 00:09:17.053
L1 measures it: the same 26 services and the same year of 500 features, under two ownership maps.

107
00:09:17.333 --> 00:09:29.643
Organised by history and layer, 91% of features needed more than one team, 26% needed three or more, and the busiest team pairs had to coordinate on 80 plus features a year.

108
00:09:29.923 --> 00:09:47.053
Aligning teams to the bounded contexts on paper improved this only modestly, 86% still crossed a team boundary, because the year's work flowed across those contexts (promotions changes always touched the cart; slot capacity always touched dark-store stock).

109
00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:55.611
The lesson: align teams to how change actually flows, which you measure, rather than to nouns or contexts inferred on a whiteboard.

110
00:09:56.961 --> 00:09:58.177
Team Topologies.

111
00:09:58.457 --> 00:10:04.601
Matthew Skelton and Manuel Pais's Team Topologies gives a vocabulary that has become standard:

112
00:10:04.881 --> 00:10:06.075
Four team types:

113
00:10:07.425 --> 00:10:08.769
Here is a quick table:

114
00:10:09.049 --> 00:10:11.460
Type, and Purpose, and Grocery example.

115
00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:12.743
Stream-aligned.

116
00:10:13.023 --> 00:10:13.833
Purpose:

117
00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:18.273
Owns a slice of the business value stream end to end; the default type.

118
00:10:18.553 --> 00:10:19.791
Grocery example:

119
00:10:20.071 --> 00:10:23.271
Checkout experience, Fulfilment, Delivery, Catalog.

120
00:10:23.551 --> 00:10:24.489
Platform.

121
00:10:24.769 --> 00:10:25.580
Purpose:

122
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:31.833
Provides internal services that reduce stream teams' cognitive load, consumed as self-service.

123
00:10:32.113 --> 00:10:33.351
Grocery example:

124
00:10:33.631 --> 00:10:37.428
Service template, CI, observability, messaging, identity.

125
00:10:37.708 --> 00:10:38.561
Enabling.

126
00:10:38.841 --> 00:10:39.652
Purpose:

127
00:10:39.932 --> 00:10:43.580
Helps stream teams adopt new capabilities, then steps away.

128
00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.097
Grocery example:

129
00:10:45.377 --> 00:10:48.897
A migration or observability enabling team (L4).

130
00:10:49.177 --> 00:10:50.628
Complicated-subsystem.

131
00:10:50.908 --> 00:10:51.719
Purpose:

132
00:10:51.999 --> 00:10:54.729
Owns a part requiring deep specialist knowledge.

133
00:10:55.009 --> 00:10:56.247
Grocery example:

134
00:10:56.527 --> 00:10:59.684
Routing optimisation, search ranking, fraud models.

135
00:11:01.034 --> 00:11:02.399
Three interaction modes:

136
00:11:02.679 --> 00:11:09.378
Collaboration, two teams work closely for a limited time on something new (high bandwidth, high cost; should end).

137
00:11:09.658 --> 00:11:18.725
X-as-a-Service, one team consumes another's capability through a clear API with minimal coordination (the goal for platform services).

138
00:11:19.005 --> 00:11:23.613
Facilitating, an enabling team coaches another team to build a capability.

139
00:11:24.963 --> 00:11:31.917
Cognitive load is the organising constraint: a team can only own as much as it can understand and operate.

140
00:11:32.197 --> 00:11:40.112
Too many services, domains or technologies per team and quality drops, on-call becomes miserable, and changes slow down.

141
00:11:40.392 --> 00:11:55.368
L2's limits (2 to 4 services per team, change load at most 1.5x the average) encode that constraint; without it, the optimal organisation is one giant team with zero hand-offs and no ability to cope.

142
00:11:55.648 --> 00:12:01.835
Team size: small, long-lived teams (5 to 9 people) with stable ownership, you build it, you run it.

143
00:12:03.185 --> 00:12:05.190
Deriving team boundaries from change.

144
00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:09.118
Because change flow is what determines coordination, measure it:

145
00:12:09.398 --> 00:12:14.838
Feature and ticket data: which services each feature touched (L1 or L2 use this).

146
00:12:15.118 --> 00:12:21.710
Change coupling from version control: files and services that change in the same commits or pull requests.

147
00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:26.470
Runtime coupling: synchronous call graphs from tracing (Ch 24).

148
00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:28.606
Then optimise under constraints.

149
00:12:28.886 --> 00:12:39.595
L2's local search assigns 26 services to 8 teams to minimise hand-offs (for each feature, teams involved minus one) within cognitive-load limits:

150
00:12:40.945 --> 00:12:42.289
Here is a quick table:

151
00:12:42.569 --> 00:12:45.897
Ownership, and Hand-offs or year, and Multi-team features.

152
00:12:46.177 --> 00:12:47.671
Historical (by layer).

153
00:12:47.951 --> 00:12:49.188
Hand-offs or year:

154
00:12:49.468 --> 00:12:50.940
591.

155
00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:52.649
Multi-team features:

156
00:12:52.929 --> 00:12:54.167
91%.

157
00:12:54.447 --> 00:12:56.196
Bounded contexts on paper.

158
00:12:56.476 --> 00:12:57.713
Hand-offs or year:

159
00:12:57.993 --> 00:12:58.975
500.

160
00:12:59.255 --> 00:13:00.684
Multi-team features:

161
00:13:00.964 --> 00:13:02.223
86%.

162
00:13:02.503 --> 00:13:04.124
Optimised from change data.

163
00:13:04.404 --> 00:13:05.641
Hand-offs or year:

164
00:13:05.921 --> 00:13:07.073
240.

165
00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:08.783
Multi-team features:

166
00:13:09.063 --> 00:13:10.343
43%.

167
00:13:11.693 --> 00:13:14.487
The optimised teams are evidence, not a decision.

168
00:13:14.767 --> 00:13:22.874
Some groupings reflect genuine domain cohesion (pricing, promotions, cart and checkout as one checkout experience team).

169
00:13:23.154 --> 00:13:39.837
Others are warnings: identity and notification grouped with rider and tracking because OTP logins and rider updates both send messages, a shared capability that belongs with a platform team as a self-service API, not inside one stream team.

170
00:13:40.117 --> 00:13:47.818
Ask of every coupling: is it essential (one domain, align a team to it) or accidental (a design flaw, fix the design)?

171
00:13:48.098 --> 00:13:52.365
And weigh skills, on-call load and domain language before moving anything.

172
00:13:52.645 --> 00:13:56.911
Re-run the analysis periodically; how change flows shifts with the product.

173
00:13:58.261 --> 00:14:00.565
Platform engineering and the paved road.

174
00:14:00.845 --> 00:14:04.920
A platform team exists to reduce stream-aligned teams' cognitive load.

175
00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:14.075
Its main product is often a paved road (golden path): the easiest way to build, ship and run a service is also the standard-compliant way.

176
00:14:14.355 --> 00:14:17.149
Components of the grocery platform's paved road:

177
00:14:17.429 --> 00:14:38.699
A service template (Backstage software template or similar) that generates a new service with the hexagonal package structure (Ch 34), the platform starter (JSON logs, tracing, metrics, outbox, Ch 15), health endpoints, an ArchUnit test (Ch 31), a CI pipeline with SBOM and vulnerability scanning,

178
00:14:38.979 --> 00:14:43.245
a Dockerfile on the standard base image, and a catalog entry with an owner.

179
00:14:44.595 --> 00:14:54.451
Self-service infrastructure: databases, topics, buckets and secrets provisioned by pull request, with policy-as-code guardrails (Ch 36).

180
00:14:54.731 --> 00:15:00.875
A service catalog or developer portal: every service, its owner, docs, dependencies, scorecard.

181
00:15:02.225 --> 00:15:05.937
The key lesson from L3: a template is a one-time copy.

182
00:15:06.217 --> 00:15:24.607
Services created from the current template passed all 8 checks; services from older templates and hand-rolled ones drifted (v2 60%, v1 28%, hand-rolled 19%), because every later improvement, SBOMs, Java 21, the newer starter, never reached them.

183
00:15:24.887 --> 00:15:28.513
Only 8 of 26 services were fully on the current path.

184
00:15:28.793 --> 00:15:33.167
So a platform team's job is not just the template but keeping the fleet on it:

185
00:15:33.447 --> 00:15:41.937
Libraries over copy-paste: put behaviour in a versioned starter or BOM that services depend on, so upgrades are a version bump.

186
00:15:43.287 --> 00:15:49.730
Automated upgrade pull requests (Renovate for versions; OpenRewrite recipes for code changes).

187
00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:55.557
Scorecards per service and team, generated by a conformance scanner, so drift is visible.

188
00:15:55.837 --> 00:16:08.957
Platform as a product: talk to users (stream teams), measure adoption and satisfaction, deprecate carefully, and keep it the thinnest viable platform, enough to reduce load, not a second bureaucracy.

189
00:16:10.307 --> 00:16:18.392
The failure mode is the ticket-ops platform: a team that provisions things on request via tickets, becoming a bottleneck with a queue.

190
00:16:18.672 --> 00:16:20.955
Self-service with guardrails is the goal.

191
00:16:22.305 --> 00:16:24.993
Governance at scale: guardrails, not gates.

192
00:16:25.273 --> 00:16:32.718
With 8 plus teams, central architecture review of every change doesn't scale (Ch 31 §2.8).

193
00:16:32.998 --> 00:16:34.171
The pattern that works:

194
00:16:34.451 --> 00:16:45.353
Principles, a short list that lets teams make consistent decisions alone (prefer events between contexts, no shared databases, personal data encrypted at the field level).

195
00:16:45.633 --> 00:16:55.105
Guardrails, automated checks that enforce the non-negotiables: fitness functions, policy-as-code, conformance scanners (L3), contract tests.

196
00:16:55.385 --> 00:16:59.886
Paved roads, make the recommended choice the default (§2.5).

197
00:17:01.236 --> 00:17:10.388
The advice process, anyone can make an architectural decision after seeking advice from those affected and from experts, recorded in an ADR.

198
00:17:10.668 --> 00:17:17.687
Light review for one-way doors, new datastores, new trust boundaries, cross-context contracts, big migrations.

199
00:17:17.967 --> 00:17:24.260
A technology radar, Adopt or Trial or Assess or Hold, so common choices need no debate.

200
00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:34.247
Architecture guild or community of practice, engineers from every team share decisions, patterns and incidents; architects facilitate rather than dictate.

201
00:17:35.597 --> 00:17:45.410
The architect's role shifts from gatekeeper to designer of the decision system: the principles, guardrails and forums that make many independent decisions coherent.

202
00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.381
Leading large migrations.

203
00:17:48.661 --> 00:17:59.115
Fleet-wide changes, framework upgrades, moving off a shared database, adopting a new messaging platform, entering cells, are where architecture leadership is most visible.

204
00:17:59.395 --> 00:18:07.757
They fail in predictable ways: under-resourced central squads, no sequencing, unclear finish lines, and a long tail nobody owns.

205
00:18:08.037 --> 00:18:16.485
L4 schedules a Spring Boot 2, then 3 or Java 21 migration across 26 services and two shared libraries:

206
00:18:17.835 --> 00:18:19.179
Here is a quick table:

207
00:18:19.459 --> 00:18:22.297
Strategy, and Finish, and Effort spent, and Notes.

208
00:18:22.577 --> 00:18:24.561
Central 2-stream migration team.

209
00:18:24.841 --> 00:18:25.630
Finish:

210
00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:28.299
31 sprints (~14 months).

211
00:18:28.579 --> 00:18:29.582
Effort spent:

212
00:18:29.862 --> 00:18:30.843
58.

213
00:18:31.123 --> 00:18:31.934
Notes:

214
00:18:32.214 --> 00:18:34.454
Bottleneck; owners don't learn their stack.

215
00:18:34.734 --> 00:18:38.254
Platform migrates libraries first, teams own services.

216
00:18:38.534 --> 00:18:39.323
Finish:

217
00:18:39.603 --> 00:18:40.755
11 sprints.

