00:19:06.846 --> 00:19:07.827
37.

234
00:19:08.107 --> 00:19:08.918
Notes:

235
00:19:09.198 --> 00:19:11.203
Mechanical changes automated.

236
00:19:12.553 --> 00:19:13.919
What the table teaches:

237
00:19:14.199 --> 00:19:21.580
Centralised migration is a bottleneck, the same work takes three times as long and leaves owning teams uninvolved.

238
00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:25.081
Sequence the dependencies and fund the enablers first.

239
00:19:25.361 --> 00:19:37.927
Starting everyone immediately looks faster and finishes at about the same time, but burns a third more effort on shims that someone must later remove, a false economy the calendar alone won't show.

240
00:19:38.207 --> 00:19:43.817
Automate the mechanical part (OpenRewrite recipes, codemods), the biggest single win.

241
00:19:45.167 --> 00:19:46.511
A migration playbook:

242
00:19:46.791 --> 00:19:52.445
Make the case with data: risk of staying (end-of-support dates, CVEs), benefit of moving.

243
00:19:52.725 --> 00:19:59.509
Plan the dependency order and the enabling work (shared libraries, recipes, docs, a reference migration).

244
00:19:59.789 --> 00:20:05.655
Team ownership: each team migrates its own services, with an enabling team available to help.

245
00:20:05.935 --> 00:20:12.719
A tracking fitness function, L3's scanner answering % of services on Boot 3, visible on a dashboard.

246
00:20:14.069 --> 00:20:19.104
A deprecation policy: announced end dates for the old path, with support tapering.

247
00:20:19.384 --> 00:20:25.315
Finish the tail: the last 10% takes disproportionate effort; plan for it and celebrate finishing.

248
00:20:26.665 --> 00:20:28.030
Technical strategy.

249
00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:35.883
Principal engineers and architects own technical strategy: a coherent answer to where is our technology going, and why?

250
00:20:36.163 --> 00:20:46.211
Diagnosis, the honest current state: constraints, risks, bottlenecks (measured where possible, L1's coordination cost, L3's drift, DORA metrics).

251
00:20:46.491 --> 00:20:55.857
Guiding policies, the few choices that address the diagnosis (teams own their services end to end, events between contexts, one paved road).

252
00:20:56.137 --> 00:21:04.841
Coherent actions, the concrete programmes: team realignment, platform investments, migrations, deprecations, each with owners and measures.

253
00:21:06.191 --> 00:21:11.780
A target architecture as a direction, not a blueprint, updated as reality teaches you.

254
00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:23.900
A technical-debt budget, a standing share of capacity (often 15 to 25%) for platform upgrades, debt paydown and resilience work, so it doesn't have to win against every feature.

255
00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:27.764
Write it down (Ch 32) and revisit it at least yearly.

256
00:21:29.114 --> 00:21:30.650
Measuring whether it's working.

257
00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:37.927
DORA metrics, deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, and time to restore service.

258
00:21:38.207 --> 00:21:43.455
They measure delivery flow and stability, and respond to team structure and platform quality.

259
00:21:43.735 --> 00:21:51.479
Coordination measures, hand-offs per feature, multi-team feature share (L1 or L2), cross-team pull-request reviews.

260
00:21:51.759 --> 00:22:00.357
Platform measures, golden-path adoption and drift (L3), time to create a production-ready service, platform satisfaction surveys.

261
00:22:00.637 --> 00:22:08.893
Architecture measures, fitness-function pass rates, drift reports (Ch 32 L4), ADR coverage of one-way doors.

262
00:22:10.243 --> 00:22:20.163
People measures, cognitive-load and on-call surveys (the SPACE framework reminds you that productivity is multi-dimensional; don't reduce it to one number).

263
00:22:20.443 --> 00:22:25.349
Use measures to find constraints and check interventions, never to rank individuals.

264
00:22:26.699 --> 00:22:29.814
Influence without authority: the staff+ engineer.

265
00:22:30.094 --> 00:22:33.998
Senior technical leaders usually lead without direct reports.

266
00:22:34.278 --> 00:22:49.617
Common staff+ archetypes (Will Larson): tech lead (guides one team's execution), architect (owns direction for a critical area), solver (dives into the hardest problems), right hand (extends an executive's reach across the org).

267
00:22:49.897 --> 00:22:51.326
All rely on influence:

268
00:22:51.606 --> 00:22:56.342
Write, design docs, strategy, ADRs; writing scales your thinking beyond meetings.

269
00:22:56.622 --> 00:23:01.657
Build consensus before the meeting, talk to the people affected; the meeting ratifies.

270
00:23:01.937 --> 00:23:07.078
Bring evidence, L1, L4-style analysis turns opinions into shared facts.

271
00:23:08.428 --> 00:23:13.399
Make others successful, sponsor, mentor, delegate the interesting work, and give credit.

272
00:23:13.679 --> 00:23:18.479
Stay close to the code, enough to keep your judgement grounded and your credibility real.

273
00:23:18.759 --> 00:23:23.985
Pick your battles, two-way doors aren't worth a fight (Ch 31); one-way doors are.

274
00:23:24.265 --> 00:23:28.831
Disagree and commit, make your case well, then support the decision once it's made.

