The architect's contribution is the plan and the enablers, not the typing: sequencing, funding the platform work first, recipes, and a tracking fitness function.

482
00:44:35.755 --> 00:44:36.693
Part 6.

483
00:44:36.973 --> 00:44:38.253
Production Concerns.

484
00:44:39.503 --> 00:44:40.655
Failure modes.

485
00:44:40.935 --> 00:44:42.279
Here is a quick table:

486
00:44:42.559 --> 00:44:44.842
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

487
00:44:45.122 --> 00:44:46.423
Teams split by layer.

488
00:44:46.703 --> 00:44:47.514
Symptom:

489
00:44:47.794 --> 00:44:50.333
Every feature needs several teams (L1).

490
00:44:50.613 --> 00:44:51.615
Mitigation:

491
00:44:51.895 --> 00:44:54.541
Stream-aligned teams by change flow (L2).

492
00:44:54.821 --> 00:44:56.997
Teams aligned to nouns, not flow.

493
00:44:57.277 --> 00:44:58.087
Symptom:

494
00:44:58.367 --> 00:45:01.845
Contexts on paper, coordination in practice (L1).

495
00:45:02.125 --> 00:45:03.127
Mitigation:

496
00:45:03.407 --> 00:45:05.498
Measure change coupling; realign.

497
00:45:05.778 --> 00:45:07.037
Overloaded teams.

498
00:45:07.317 --> 00:45:08.127
Symptom:

499
00:45:08.407 --> 00:45:11.522
Slow changes, painful on-call, quality drops.

500
00:45:11.802 --> 00:45:12.805
Mitigation:

501
00:45:13.085 --> 00:45:16.519
Cognitive-load limits; platform takes shared capabilities.

502
00:45:16.799 --> 00:45:17.823
Template drift.

503
00:45:18.103 --> 00:45:18.914
Symptom:

504
00:45:19.194 --> 00:45:21.967
Old services miss every improvement (L3).

505
00:45:22.247 --> 00:45:23.250
Mitigation:

506
00:45:23.530 --> 00:45:27.199
Versioned libraries, automated upgrade PRs, scorecards.

507
00:45:27.479 --> 00:45:28.845
Ticket-ops platform.

508
00:45:29.125 --> 00:45:29.935
Symptom:

509
00:45:30.215 --> 00:45:33.095
Queues and workarounds (§3.3).

510
00:45:33.375 --> 00:45:34.378
Mitigation:

511
00:45:34.658 --> 00:45:37.730
Self-service with guardrails; platform as product.

512
00:45:39.080 --> 00:45:40.637
Central migration squad.

513
00:45:40.917 --> 00:45:41.728
Symptom:

514
00:45:42.008 --> 00:45:44.312
Migration never finishes (L4).

515
00:45:44.592 --> 00:45:45.595
Mitigation:

516
00:45:45.875 --> 00:45:48.947
Platform-first enablers, team ownership, codemods.

517
00:45:49.227 --> 00:45:51.147
Architecture review bottleneck.

518
00:45:51.427 --> 00:45:52.237
Symptom:

519
00:45:52.517 --> 00:45:54.181
Teams bypass architects.

520
00:45:54.461 --> 00:45:55.464
Mitigation:

521
00:45:55.744 --> 00:45:58.240
Advice process, principles, guardrails.

522
00:45:58.520 --> 00:46:00.227
Reorg without architecture.

523
00:46:00.507 --> 00:46:01.317
Symptom:

524
00:46:01.597 --> 00:46:03.325
New seams where the old ones were.

525
00:46:03.605 --> 00:46:04.608
Mitigation:

526
00:46:04.888 --> 00:46:08.365
Inverse Conway: design teams and architecture together.

527
00:46:09.715 --> 00:46:11.913
Observability of the organisation.

528
00:46:12.193 --> 00:46:16.161
DORA metrics per team from CI or CD and incident data.

529
00:46:16.441 --> 00:46:25.294
Coordination: multi-team features and cross-team PR reviews per quarter (L1 or L2 method on real ticket and git data).

530
00:46:25.574 --> 00:46:31.270
Paved-road adoption: scanner scorecards per service and team (L3), trend over time.

531
00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:38.569
Migration progress: a fitness function per programme (% migrated), with the tail listed by owner (L4).

532
00:46:38.849 --> 00:46:44.353
Surveys: cognitive load, on-call health, platform satisfaction, quarterly and anonymous.

533
00:46:45.703 --> 00:46:46.641
Security.

534
00:46:46.921 --> 00:46:50.505
The paved road is the most effective security control at scale:

535
00:46:50.785 --> 00:47:00.023
SBOMs, vulnerability scanning, secure defaults and patched base images reach every service automatically, or don't reach drifted ones (L3).

536
00:47:00.303 --> 00:47:08.921
Ownership in the catalog is a security requirement: every service has someone who receives its vulnerability alerts and on-call pages.

537
00:47:09.201 --> 00:47:16.220
Migrations are often security-driven (end-of-support frameworks); track them like vulnerabilities, with deadlines.

538
00:47:17.570 --> 00:47:18.893
Performance and flow.

539
00:47:19.173 --> 00:47:30.991
Team size and ownership affect delivery speed more than most technical choices: every extra team on a feature adds hand-off latency (L1's model assumes 4 days; measure your own).

540
00:47:31.271 --> 00:47:37.799
Collaboration mode is expensive; time-box it and move to X-as-a-service once the interface is clear.

541
00:47:38.079 --> 00:47:44.522
Platform APIs need SLOs like any product, a slow or flaky platform taxes every team.

542
00:47:45.872 --> 00:47:46.597
Cost.

543
00:47:46.877 --> 00:47:59.357
A platform team costs headcount; it pays back through fewer hand-offs, faster service creation, fewer incidents from drift, and migrations that finish (L4's 31, then 8.6 sprints).

544
00:47:59.637 --> 00:48:07.360
Reorganisations have real costs (context switching, lost knowledge, morale); do them deliberately, rarely, and with evidence.

545
00:48:07.640 --> 00:48:15.832
Allocate a standing share of capacity to platform and debt work (§2.8), or it loses every prioritisation battle.

546
00:48:17.182 --> 00:48:18.121
Part 7.

547
00:48:18.401 --> 00:48:20.107
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

548
00:48:21.357 --> 00:48:22.275
Watch out:

549
00:48:22.555 --> 00:48:23.195
1.

550
00:48:23.475 --> 00:48:24.861
Ignoring Conway's law.

551
00:48:25.141 --> 00:48:32.459
Drawing a target architecture without changing team boundaries; the org chart rewrites it (§1.1, L1).

552
00:48:33.809 --> 00:48:34.726
Watch out:

553
00:48:35.006 --> 00:48:35.710
2.

554
00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:37.633
Teams by technical layer.

555
00:48:37.913 --> 00:48:42.883
Frontend team, backend team, database team: every feature crosses all of them.

556
00:48:44.233 --> 00:48:45.151
Watch out:

557
00:48:45.431 --> 00:48:46.177
3.

558
00:48:46.457 --> 00:48:48.889
Aligning teams to nouns rather than flow.

559
00:48:49.169 --> 00:48:54.396
Contexts that look right on paper but don't match how work actually changes (L1).

560
00:48:54.676 --> 00:48:55.977
Measure change coupling.

561
00:48:57.327 --> 00:48:58.245
Watch out:

562
00:48:58.525 --> 00:48:59.229
4.

563
00:48:59.509 --> 00:49:01.002
One team owning too much.

564
00:49:01.282 --> 00:49:10.477
Minimising hand-offs by piling services onto one team trades coordination cost for cognitive overload (L2's degenerate case).

565
00:49:11.827 --> 00:49:12.744
Watch out:

566
00:49:13.024 --> 00:49:13.792
5.

567
00:49:14.072 --> 00:49:15.672
Architects as gatekeepers.

568
00:49:15.952 --> 00:49:19.920
Central review of everything becomes a bottleneck and gets bypassed.

569
00:49:20.200 --> 00:49:22.355
Guardrails, principles and advice.

570
00:49:23.705 --> 00:49:24.622
Watch out:

571
00:49:24.902 --> 00:49:25.649
6.

572
00:49:25.929 --> 00:49:27.721
Standards as wiki pages.

573
00:49:28.001 --> 00:49:32.161
What isn't the default doesn't happen consistently (§1.1).

574
00:49:32.441 --> 00:49:34.425
Paved roads and automated checks.

575
00:49:35.775 --> 00:49:36.692
Watch out:

576
00:49:36.972 --> 00:49:37.719
7.

577
00:49:37.999 --> 00:49:39.535
Templates without upgrades.

578
00:49:39.815 --> 00:49:43.697
A template is a one-time copy; the fleet drifts from it (L3).

579
00:49:43.977 --> 00:49:46.644
Versioned libraries and automated PRs.

580
00:49:47.994 --> 00:49:48.911
Watch out:

581
00:49:49.191 --> 00:49:49.874
8.

582
00:49:50.154 --> 00:49:51.818
Ticket-ops platform teams.

583
00:49:52.098 --> 00:49:55.831
A platform that provisions by ticket is a queue, not a product.

584
00:49:56.111 --> 00:49:57.882
Self-service with guardrails.

585
00:49:59.232 --> 00:50:00.149
Watch out:

586
00:50:00.429 --> 00:50:01.176
9.

587
00:50:01.456 --> 00:50:03.333
The two-person migration squad.

588
00:50:03.613 --> 00:50:09.224
Central teams can't migrate a fleet in reasonable time, and owners learn nothing (L4).

589
00:50:10.574 --> 00:50:11.491
Watch out:

590
00:50:11.771 --> 00:50:12.475
10.

591
00:50:12.755 --> 00:50:15.422
Starting everyone before the enablers are ready.

592
00:50:15.702 --> 00:50:21.121
Looks faster, finishes about the same, costs a third more effort in workarounds (L4).

593
00:50:22.471 --> 00:50:23.388
Watch out:

594
00:50:23.668 --> 00:50:24.500
11.

595
00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:26.188
Measuring individuals.

596
00:50:26.468 --> 00:50:31.033
DORA and flow metrics are for finding system constraints, not ranking people.

597
00:50:32.383 --> 00:50:33.301
Watch out:

598
00:50:33.581 --> 00:50:34.370
12.

599
00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:36.485
Reorganising without evidence.

600
00:50:36.765 --> 00:50:39.965
Frequent, intuition-driven reorgs destroy ownership.

601
00:50:40.245 --> 00:50:44.447
Use change data and cognitive-load limits, then keep teams stable.

602
00:50:45.797 --> 00:50:46.672
Part 8.

603
00:50:46.952 --> 00:50:48.296
Interview Q and A.

604
00:50:49.546 --> 00:50:50.378
Beginner.

605
00:50:50.658 --> 00:50:51.682
Question 1.

606
00:50:51.962 --> 00:50:53.199
What is Conway's law?

607
00:50:53.479 --> 00:50:54.845
Thirty-second answer:

608
00:50:55.125 --> 00:50:59.562
Organisations design systems that mirror their communication structures.

609
00:50:59.842 --> 00:51:13.261
If teams are organised by technical layer, the system grows layer-shaped seams and every feature crosses teams; if teams own business capabilities end to end, the system tends toward capability-shaped services.

610
00:51:13.541 --> 00:51:19.898
The inverse Conway manoeuvre uses this deliberately: design the teams you need for the architecture you want.

611
00:51:21.248 --> 00:51:22.251
Question 2.

612
00:51:22.531 --> 00:51:25.197
What are the four team types in Team Topologies?

613
00:51:25.477 --> 00:51:26.843
Thirty-second answer:

614
00:51:27.123 --> 00:51:48.477
Stream-aligned teams own a slice of the value stream end to end (the default); platform teams provide internal self-service capabilities that reduce stream teams' cognitive load; enabling teams coach other teams to adopt new capabilities and then step away; complicated-subsystem teams own parts needing deep specialist knowledge.

615
00:51:49.827 --> 00:51:50.873
Question 3.

616
00:51:51.153 --> 00:51:53.286
What is a paved road (golden path)?

617
00:51:53.566 --> 00:51:54.931
Thirty-second answer:

618
00:51:55.211 --> 00:52:08.737
A supported, well-maintained default way to build and run services, a template, shared libraries, CI pipelines, infrastructure modules and docs, that makes the standard-compliant choice the easiest choice.

619
00:52:09.017 --> 00:52:12.281
Teams can leave it with good reason, but most don't need to.

620
00:52:13.631 --> 00:52:14.697
Question 4.

621
00:52:14.977 --> 00:52:16.364
What are the DORA metrics?

622
00:52:16.644 --> 00:52:18.009
Thirty-second answer:

623
00:52:18.289 --> 00:52:29.553
Four measures of software delivery performance: deployment frequency, lead time for changes (commit to production), change failure rate, and time to restore service after a failure.

624
00:52:29.833 --> 00:52:36.788
Together they capture throughput and stability, and they improve with good team boundaries, automation and platform quality.

625
00:52:38.138 --> 00:52:39.141
Intermediate.

626
00:52:39.421 --> 00:52:40.530
Question 5.

627
00:52:40.810 --> 00:52:42.645
How do you decide team boundaries?

628
00:52:42.925 --> 00:52:44.290
Thirty-second answer:

629
00:52:44.570 --> 00:52:53.743
Start from how change flows: which services change together (feature and git data), which bounded contexts exist, and which capabilities are shared.

630
00:52:54.023 --> 00:53:02.685
Group services so most features need one team, within cognitive-load limits (a few services per team, balanced change and on-call load).

631
00:53:02.965 --> 00:53:06.570
Move shared capabilities to a platform team as self-service.

632
00:53:06.850 --> 00:53:10.626
Validate with domain language and skills, then keep teams stable.

633
00:53:11.976 --> 00:53:13.085
Question 6.

634
00:53:13.365 --> 00:53:17.227
What is team cognitive load and why does it matter for architecture?

635
00:53:17.507 --> 00:53:18.872
Thirty-second answer:

636
00:53:19.152 --> 00:53:27.237
The total mental effort a team needs to understand, change and operate what it owns, domains, services, technologies, on-call.

637
00:53:27.517 --> 00:53:33.299
When it exceeds capacity, changes slow, quality drops and on-call becomes unsustainable.

638
00:53:33.579 --> 00:53:43.136
Architecture should be split so each team's share fits; platforms exist to remove undifferentiated load (infrastructure, observability, messaging).

639
00:53:44.486 --> 00:53:45.574
Question 7.

640
00:53:45.854 --> 00:53:49.886
How do you keep 100 services consistent without central control?

641
00:53:50.166 --> 00:53:51.531
Thirty-second answer:

642
00:53:51.811 --> 00:54:06.403
Put the standard into defaults and automation: a service template, shared versioned libraries (logging, tracing, security, outbox), a CI pipeline, infrastructure modules, and a conformance scanner that produces scorecards.

643
00:54:06.683 --> 00:54:09.585
Automate upgrades with Renovate or OpenRewrite.

644
00:54:09.865 --> 00:54:14.707
Publish principles and a tech radar for decisions the paved road doesn't cover.

645
00:54:16.057 --> 00:54:17.081
Question 8.

646
00:54:17.361 --> 00:54:19.324
What makes a platform team successful?

647
00:54:19.604 --> 00:54:20.969
Thirty-second answer:

648
00:54:21.249 --> 00:54:38.337
Treating the platform as a product for internal users: understand stream teams' needs, provide self-service capabilities with clear APIs and SLOs, measure adoption and satisfaction, keep it the thinnest viable platform, and maintain it (upgrades, deprecations).

649
00:54:38.617 --> 00:54:41.412
It should reduce cognitive load, not add gates.

650
00:54:42.762 --> 00:54:43.829
Question 9.

651
00:54:44.109 --> 00:54:47.053
How do architects govern without becoming a bottleneck?

652
00:54:47.333 --> 00:54:48.698
Thirty-second answer:

653
00:54:48.978 --> 00:55:01.458
Replace review gates with principles, guardrails (fitness functions, policy-as-code, scanners), paved roads, and the advice process, teams decide after seeking advice, and record ADRs.

654
00:55:01.738 --> 00:55:04.063
Reserve light review for one-way doors.

655
00:55:04.343 --> 00:55:08.098
Architects facilitate a guild and spend time embedded with teams.

656
00:55:09.448 --> 00:55:10.216
Senior.

657
00:55:10.496 --> 00:55:11.541
Question 10.

658
00:55:11.821 --> 00:55:16.707
How would you lead a Spring Boot 2, then 3 migration across 100 services?

659
00:55:16.987 --> 00:55:18.352
Thirty-second answer:

660
00:55:18.632 --> 00:55:34.525
Make the case (support dates, CVEs), map dependencies, fund the enablers first (shared libraries migrated, OpenRewrite recipes, a reference migration by one team), then let each team own its services with an enabling team available.

661
00:55:34.805 --> 00:55:42.763
Track a fitness function (% of services on Boot 3) on a dashboard, announce an end date for the old path, and plan the long tail.

662
00:55:44.113 --> 00:55:45.243
Question 11.

663
00:55:45.523 --> 00:55:47.721
Your target architecture keeps drifting.

664
00:55:48.001 --> 00:55:49.857
How do you diagnose and fix it?

665
00:55:50.137 --> 00:55:51.502
Thirty-second answer:

666
00:55:51.782 --> 00:55:58.097
Check the socio-technical causes first: do team boundaries match the architecture's boundaries (Conway)?

667
00:55:58.377 --> 00:56:00.510
Are standards defaults or documents?

668
00:56:00.790 --> 00:56:02.817
Are rules enforced by guardrails?

669
00:56:03.097 --> 00:56:09.390
Measure coordination (multi-team features), drift (fitness functions, dependency drift), and adoption.

670
00:56:09.670 --> 00:56:15.899
Then fix structurally: realign teams to change flow, strengthen the paved road, add guardrails.

671
00:56:17.249 --> 00:56:18.337
Question 12.

672
00:56:18.617 --> 00:56:23.119
When should two teams collaborate closely, and when should they interact as a service?

673
00:56:23.399 --> 00:56:24.764
Thirty-second answer:

674
00:56:25.044 --> 00:56:35.092
Collaborate (high-bandwidth, joint work) when discovering something new with an unclear interface, a new capability, a redesign across a boundary, and time-box it.

675
00:56:35.372 --> 00:56:44.375
Once the interface is clear, move to X-as-a-service: one team provides a stable API, the other consumes it with minimal coordination.

676
00:56:45.725 --> 00:56:47.005
Question 13.

677
00:56:47.285 --> 00:56:50.826
How do you handle ownership transfers during a reorganisation?

678
00:56:51.106 --> 00:56:52.471
Thirty-second answer:

679
00:56:52.751 --> 00:57:09.967
Treat each service transfer as a migration: a handover checklist (runbooks, alerts, dashboards, known issues, secrets, on-call shadowing), a period of joint on-call, updated catalog ownership, and a scorecard check (L3) so the receiving team knows what it inherits.

680
00:57:10.247 --> 00:57:13.853
Transfer fewer, larger chunks rather than scattering services.

681
00:57:15.203 --> 00:57:16.397
Question 14.

682
00:57:16.677 --> 00:57:20.496
How do you prioritise platform and tech-debt work against features?

683
00:57:20.776 --> 00:57:22.141
Thirty-second answer:

684
00:57:22.421 --> 00:57:38.208
Give it a standing capacity allocation (often 15 to 25%) rather than competing item by item, and back specific large investments with data: incidents caused, lead-time impact, security exposure, migration deadlines.

685
00:57:38.488 --> 00:57:42.307
Frame debt as risk and cost in business terms, and show results.

686
00:57:43.657 --> 00:57:44.553
Architect.

687
00:57:44.833 --> 00:57:46.027
Question 15.

688
00:57:46.307 --> 00:57:51.790
Design the engineering organisation for a 150-engineer grocery platform.

689
00:57:52.070 --> 00:57:53.435
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:57:53.715 --> 00:58:13.726
15 to 18 stream-aligned teams of 6 to 9 grouped around value streams (checkout, orders and payments, catalog and discovery, store operations, inventory, delivery, customer), boundaries validated with change data and cognitive-load limits; a platform group (developer platform, data platform,

691
00:58:14.006 --> 00:58:23.478
messaging or identity or notification as services); one or two enabling teams; complicated-subsystem teams for routing and search ranking.

692
00:58:23.758 --> 00:58:30.819
Govern with principles, paved roads, guardrails and an advice process; measure DORA, coordination and adoption.

693
00:58:32.169 --> 00:58:33.364
Question 16.

694
00:58:33.644 --> 00:58:36.993
How do you write and drive a multi-year technical strategy?

695
00:58:37.273 --> 00:58:38.639
Thirty-second answer:

696
00:58:38.919 --> 00:58:50.183
Diagnose honestly with data (coordination costs, drift, reliability, DORA), choose a few guiding policies, and define coherent actions with owners, sequencing and measures.

697
00:58:50.463 --> 00:58:59.188
Write it down, socialise it before publishing, tie it to business goals, allocate capacity, and review it at least yearly as evidence changes.

698
00:59:00.538 --> 00:59:01.882
Question 17.

699
00:59:02.162 --> 00:59:05.959
How do you lead without authority across teams that don't report to you?

700
00:59:06.239 --> 00:59:07.605
Thirty-second answer:

701
00:59:07.885 --> 00:59:25.741
Earn influence through evidence, writing, and making others successful: bring data that reframes problems, write clear proposals, build consensus before meetings, sponsor and mentor, stay close to the code, choose battles (one-way doors), and disagree-and-commit once decisions are made.

702
00:59:26.021 --> 00:59:29.050
Align your work with leaders' goals so they want your help.

703
00:59:30.400 --> 00:59:31.573
Question 18.

704
00:59:31.853 --> 00:59:35.907
Centralised platform versus team autonomy, where do you draw the line?

705
00:59:36.187 --> 00:59:37.552
Thirty-second answer:

706
00:59:37.832 --> 00:59:57.139
Centralise what's undifferentiated and where consistency matters (build and deploy, observability, security baselines, identity, messaging, data governance); leave to teams what's specific to their domain (domain models, service design within principles, feature technology choices within the radar).

707
00:59:57.419 --> 01:00:03.819
Make the central parts self-service and optional-but-attractive, with guardrails only for true non-negotiables.

708
01:00:05.169 --> 01:00:06.406
Question 19.

709
01:00:06.686 --> 01:00:09.630
How would you evaluate whether a reorganisation worked?

710
01:00:09.910 --> 01:00:11.275
Thirty-second answer:

711
01:00:11.555 --> 01:00:25.721
Define measures before it: multi-team features and hand-offs (L1 or L2 method on real data), DORA metrics per team, incident and on-call load, cognitive-load survey results, and golden-path adoption.

712
01:00:26.001 --> 01:00:32.721
Compare over two to three quarters, accounting for the dip during transition, and check qualitative signals from teams.

713
01:00:34.071 --> 01:00:35.180
Question 20.

714
01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:39.769
What distinguishes a principal engineer's impact from a senior engineer's?

715
01:00:40.049 --> 01:00:41.415
Thirty-second answer:

716
01:00:41.695 --> 01:00:42.975
Scope and leverage.

717
01:00:43.255 --> 01:00:46.775
A senior engineer delivers excellent systems within a team.

718
01:00:47.055 --> 01:01:02.223
A principal engineer shapes how many teams build systems: team boundaries aligned to change, platforms that make good choices easy, governance that scales, migrations that finish, and a strategy that connects technology to business outcomes.

719
01:01:02.503 --> 01:01:05.233
Their output is measured in other teams' results.

720
01:01:06.583 --> 01:01:07.479
Part 9.

721
01:01:07.759 --> 01:01:09.167
Summary and Practice.

722
01:01:10.417 --> 01:01:11.505
Key takeaways.

723
01:01:11.785 --> 01:01:19.785
Architecture at scale is socio-technical: team boundaries, defaults and incentives shape the system (§2.1).

724
01:01:20.065 --> 01:01:26.977
Conway's law is a force: align teams to how change actually flows, not to layers or nouns (L1).

725
01:01:27.257 --> 01:01:41.999
Use Team Topologies' vocabulary: stream-aligned, platform, enabling and complicated-subsystem teams; collaboration, X-as-a-service and facilitating modes; cognitive load as the constraint (§2.3).

726
01:01:42.279 --> 01:01:49.319
Derive boundaries from change data within load limits, and treat the result as evidence for human judgement (L2).

727
01:01:50.669 --> 01:01:57.943
Run the paved road as a product and keep the fleet on it: versioned libraries, automated upgrades, scorecards (L3).

728
01:01:58.223 --> 01:02:04.815
Govern with principles, guardrails and advice, reserving review for one-way doors (§2.6).

729
01:02:05.095 --> 01:02:12.903
Lead migrations with a plan and enablers: platform first, team ownership, codemods, a tracking fitness function (L4).

730
01:02:13.183 --> 01:02:17.301
Measure flow and adoption, not individuals (§2.9).

731
01:02:17.581 --> 01:02:22.253
Lead through evidence, writing and others' success (§2.10).

732
01:02:23.603 --> 01:02:24.925
Decision cheat sheet.

733
01:02:25.205 --> 01:02:26.549
Here is a quick table:

734
01:02:26.829 --> 01:02:28.493
Situation, and Approach.

735
01:02:28.773 --> 01:02:30.715
Features constantly cross teams:

736
01:02:30.995 --> 01:02:35.069
Measure change coupling; realign teams to flow (L1, L2).

737
01:02:35.349 --> 01:02:37.205
A capability everyone needs:

738
01:02:37.485 --> 01:02:39.533
Platform team, X-as-a-service.

739
01:02:39.813 --> 01:02:41.627
Teams need to adopt something new:

740
01:02:41.907 --> 01:02:44.147
Enabling team, facilitating mode.

741
01:02:44.427 --> 01:02:46.027
Deep specialist component:

742
01:02:46.307 --> 01:02:48.035
Complicated-subsystem team.

743
01:02:48.315 --> 01:02:50.427
Services configured inconsistently:

744
01:02:50.707 --> 01:02:55.933
Paved road + versioned libraries + scanner + auto-upgrade PRs (L3).

745
01:02:56.213 --> 01:02:57.451
Fleet-wide upgrade:

746
01:02:57.731 --> 01:03:01.933
Platform-first enablers, team ownership, codemods, tracking (L4).

747
01:03:02.213 --> 01:03:03.664
Architecture review queue:

748
01:03:03.944 --> 01:03:06.589
Principles + guardrails + advice process.

749
01:03:06.869 --> 01:03:08.235
Is the reorg working?:

750
01:03:08.515 --> 01:03:12.184
DORA, hand-offs per feature, surveys over 2 to 3 quarters.

751
01:03:13.534 --> 01:03:14.665
Exercises.

752
01:03:14.945 --> 01:03:20.363
(B3) Export six months of your team's tickets or PRs with the services each touched.

753
01:03:20.643 --> 01:03:25.657
Run L1's analysis on them: what share of changes needs more than one team?

754
01:03:25.937 --> 01:03:31.419
(B3) In L2, change the limits to 3 to 5 services per team and 6 teams.

755
01:03:31.699 --> 01:03:33.961
How do hand-offs and max load change?

756
01:03:34.241 --> 01:03:35.606
Where would you draw the line?

757
01:03:35.886 --> 01:03:45.635
(B3) Add two checks to L3's scanner, uses the standard base image digest and has a runbook link in catalog-info, and compute per-team scorecards.

758
01:03:46.985 --> 01:03:55.583
(B4) In L4, give the platform team two parallel streams and add a reference migration task that must finish before other teams start.

759
01:03:55.863 --> 01:03:57.484
Does it change the finish date?

760
01:03:57.764 --> 01:03:58.660
The effort?

761
01:03:58.940 --> 01:04:09.159
(B4) Write a one-page technical strategy for your organisation using diagnosis or guiding policies or coherent actions, with one measure per action.

